<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Max Havelaar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Max Havelaar, or the Coffee Auctions of the Dutch Trading Company</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Max Havelaar is a novel published in 1860 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multatuli">Multatuli</a>, the pen name of the former colonial official Eduard Douwes Dekker. Drawing on his service in Lebak on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, it attacks the exploitation of Javanese peasants under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> cultivation system and the local rulers who served it. It caused a sensation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and contributed to the later reform of colonial policy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/max-havelaar.jpg" alt="Max Havelaar" width="212" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eduard Douwes Dekker; cover designer unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Max_Havelaar_9e_druk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
